1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,644
Welcome to Atomic Education.

2
00:00:02,703 --> 00:00:09,808
This lesson was written by the Atomic teaching team to help you learn Cambridge Lower Secondary Science with confidence.

3
00:00:09,808 --> 00:00:10,513
Let's begin!

4
00:00:11,571 --> 00:00:14,214
Welcome to Unit 13: Earth and Climate Change.

5
00:00:15,272 --> 00:00:19,852
Over millions of years, Earth's surface has changed as tectonic plates moved.

6
00:00:19,852 --> 00:00:25,783
Today, the climate is changing in ways never seen before, and rising seas are eating away coastlines.

7
00:00:26,842 --> 00:00:33,301
A purple crystal of potassium manganate(VII) is placed at the bottom of a large beaker of water, at one side.

8
00:00:33,301 --> 00:00:39,701
The beaker is heated gently under that side only. Predict: where will the purple colour go? Pause and decide.

9
00:00:40,760 --> 00:00:45,164
Watch. A purple stream rises straight up from the crystal, above the flame.

10
00:00:46,223 --> 00:00:51,625
At the top it moves across, sinks down the far side and flows back along the bottom: a loop.

11
00:00:52,683 --> 00:00:57,264
Explain what you saw. The heated water expands, becomes less dense and rises.

12
00:00:57,264 --> 00:01:03,429
At the top it cools, becomes denser and sinks, so the water moves round in a loop: a convection current.

13
00:01:03,429 --> 00:01:09,360
Hot rock in Earth's mantle moves in the same way, very slowly, carrying the tectonic plates with it.

14
00:01:09,360 --> 00:01:11,650
Pause and explain it in your own words.

15
00:01:12,708 --> 00:01:15,821
Earth's crust is made of huge rigid tectonic plates.

16
00:01:15,821 --> 00:01:21,693
Below, in the mantle, hot magma rises, cools near the crust, and sinks again: a convection current.

17
00:01:21,693 --> 00:01:24,276
These currents drag the plates slowly along.

18
00:01:25,335 --> 00:01:29,328
Where plates push together, rocks fold upwards into fold mountains.

19
00:01:29,328 --> 00:01:33,203
Where they pull apart or slide under one another, volcanoes form.

20
00:01:33,203 --> 00:01:37,020
And where they suddenly slip past each other, we get earthquakes.
